                <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
                  Mobile camera snaps upload via streaming Multer memory buffers (max 25MB limit). Sharp reads EXIF orientation tags and executes <code>.rotate()</code>, ensuring photos taken on iOS and Android devices never appear sideways on static sites.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="retro-card p-4 m-0 flex flex-col gap-2">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-bold text-xs text-prompt">
                  <span>🖼️</span>
                  <span>2. SHARP WEBP COMPRESSION</span>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
                  Images scale to a max 1200px boundary (main image) and 400x400px square thumbnail. Converted to WebP format (quality 85), saving 70-80% bandwidth over raw JPEGs while keeping images around 80-150KB.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="retro-card p-4 m-0 flex flex-col gap-2">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-bold text-xs text-prompt">
                  <span>⚡</span>
                  <span>3. ELEVENTY v3 FIFO QUEUE</span>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
                  Static galleries compile inside isolated user folders (<code>dist/static-galleries/&lt;userId&gt;/</code>). Build tasks serialize through an in-memory FIFO BuildQueue, eliminating template race conditions.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="retro-card p-4 m-0 flex flex-col gap-2">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-bold text-xs text-prompt">
                  <span>🗑️</span>
                  <span>4. ZERO-RETENTION LOCAL PURGE</span>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
                  As soon as Neocities confirms upload success, local copies of user images are immediately wiped from disk (<code>fs.rm(uploadsDir)</code>). The Neogram server retains 0 bytes of user media.
                </p>
              </div>

